<p>In Israel, where I grew up, this was a sacred time in the day. You weren&rsquo;t allowed to play soccer with your friends; you weren&rsquo;t allowed to make noise. In kindergarten, you&rsquo;re &ldquo;strongly encouraged&rdquo; to take a nap, or else.</p>
<p>This is slowly becoming a sacred time for me too. As time goes by, my struggle with the need for a &ldquo;power nap&rdquo; after 4.5 to 6.5 hours of night sleep is becoming something I&rsquo;m comfortable with, a ritual I&rsquo;m looking forward to. As a morning person whose creativity mostly takes place in the morning with a cup of coffee, which is why I blog in these hours usually (you&rsquo;re welcome), it dawned on me last week that with a Shnatz, I get <em>two</em> mornings in one day. When I wake up from my nap, which is usually about 20 minutes, I&rsquo;m energetic enough to exercise or tackle the more challenging tasks at work.</p>
<p>For too long, I&rsquo;ve treated my need for an afternoon nap as a weakness or a problem. It&rsquo;s been an issue I have to deal with, bundled with my insomnia. I started looking for a medical intervention but stopped at the last minute and asked myself for the 100th time: &ldquo;Is taking a short nap <em>really</em> that bad, or are you just led to believe it is?&rdquo;</p>
<p>I started to embrace it. It was hard, but I managed to turn off my phone for 30 minutes (isn&rsquo;t that nuts when you think about it?) during my workday. Why 30 minutes? I give myself 5 minutes to fall asleep and 5 minutes to fully wake up.</p>
<p>No surprise, it&rsquo;s working well - the difference is that now I feel better about it. I&rsquo;m going to start scheduling it in my calendar. I&rsquo;m going to stop being ashamed of it and start calling it a &ldquo;life hack&rdquo; instead, even though some of my friends call it &ldquo;you&rsquo;re just getting older, ya old fart.&rdquo; Whatever! The result is the same.</p>
<p>This break is useful for additional things. One of those is to signal the time for exercise, as indicated earlier. I exercise at home, so I can do other things in between sets. I can also use this time to cook for myself, which I enjoy. A quick second shower, especially on hot days, is also a good idea, and with it, a good time to charge my Apple Watch. At the very least, it&rsquo;s a good time to &ldquo;reboot&rdquo; my brain (hmm&hellip; a chance for meditation? Not a bad idea) and let my internal programming start fresh, with a new set of priorities.</p>
<p>If you get tired in the afternoon, I recommend you try it if you can escape somewhere for even 10 minutes. It doesn&rsquo;t have to happen at exactly the same hour on the same day. I take a nap in between meetings or instead of my lunch (don&rsquo;t need that much time to eat anyway). Sometimes I wake up rested after less than 10 minutes, other times I want to keep sleeping - but I find that <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polyphasic_sleep">20 minutes or so is the sweet spot</a>, and I usually wake without an alarm. So give yourself a Shnatz. You&rsquo;re worth it.</p>
